ld/id #uuid "f83f97ce-cbc9-58c2-bc53-a272b5b0ea58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146498} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89d8404e-4aaf-511f-9859-6d27274954ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403113} {:answer/id #uuid "9862b857-6ea9-52a3-a713-1d7ee260467b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403114} {:answer/id #uuid "bbf5884c-aaa5-5d80-8e2b-0a81f4471125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403115} {:answer/id #uuid "5c20f220-acdf-509c-abcb-d33e114a770c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403116} {:answer/id #uuid "796ba2c3-bb1b-5078-8683-f57cc7daf2a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "796ba2c3-bb1b-5078-8683-f57cc7daf2a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403117} :answer-field/id #uuid "96536a92-d62c-5223-bb4d-c2d51d3aea5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c92b66b-ccec-5e6d-8f75-9e3b9cdb1f7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-64207" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $P(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8$\nII. $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-2$\nIII. $2x+7$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=4{y}^{3}.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{y}^{3})=0\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8)=0=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-3)=0=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-3$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=2x+7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+7)=0=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2x+7$ gives an exact equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Mass-Spring System" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050824 :topic/math-academy-id 2524 :topic/title "Simple Harmonic Oscillators"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82853144-4c9e-5e1c-ae91-afba29c38855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146529} {:answer/id #uuid "9f64e93b-fd23-5399-9ce1-9eb5197f1cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146530} {:answer/id #uuid "cbd9268f-819e-5411-8cb2-2ab284f63d38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146531} {:answer/id #uuid "5ffc1efc-e5bf-5b30-b1bf-c52838f919c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146532} {:answer/id #uuid "7ebde10b-2eb4-51e4-a03c-db8054846660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146533}] :answer-field/id #uuid "be494fe8-41ae-57df-b0d5-dbf058fc6ada" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146528}] :question/id #uuid "5ca151e3-9176-5b95-836c-229156802261" :question/math-academy-id "q-64223" :question/problem "A particle of mass $5kg$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 20kg/s^{2}$. The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t > 0$ is the time in seconds.\n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The governing differential equation is $y^{″} + 4y = 0$\n2. The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f = \\frac{2}{π}s^{-1}$\n3. The general solution can be expressed as $y = R\\cos (2t + ϕ)$, where $R$ is the amplitude and $ϕ$ is the phase."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e17fe7fc-3b85-5f2c-aa69-2336b05b16b2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Simple Harmonic Motion Equations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050824 :topic/math-academy-id 2524 :topic/title "Simple Harmonic Oscillators"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8fe04a6-f3f1-5d84-9cd5-296dd673db80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.ty