e {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149165}] :question/id #uuid "21fc8370-f920-5f93-ad85-7645ec8ff298" :question/math-academy-id "q-171850" :question/problem "$P(x)$: $10 \\mid x$ and $x$ is odd\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $Z$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The truth set of $P(x)$ is $∅$.\n2. The truth set of $P(x)$ is $Z$.\n3. $∃x ∈ Z, P(x)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6927f03c-aa7a-56ac-8cd0-c036a11aadc9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Truth Value of a Statement Involving Nested Quantifiers" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050691 :topic/math-academy-id 2792 :topic/title "Nested Quantifiers"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "caa06155-7ec7-57be-b3e1-5f4d0552519b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149226} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c3fe9e-4742-5fc6-8c4c-fcffc5c03582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149227} {:answer/id #uuid "f33f5553-fcb5-5895-a4d2-335624521ac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149228} {:answer/id #uuid "17403e39-4d30-54a3-9a14-1525138521e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149229} {:answer/id #uuid "4ca2d674-2bd5-526c-ac6e-e4f494eadc13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149230}] :answer-field/id #uuid "36d6866a-df7a-5d39-9638-32644089fd65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149225} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a552ec64-d8f6-53ae-9ab2-593d971836a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381449} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d75b4e-523d-5d82-b21d-8b38e7e0f7c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381450} {:answer/id #uuid "a09b9e0a-fb52-572c-ba07-497a7045bfd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381451} {:answer/id #uuid "c54b98ac-4438-57c8-9c88-677e9832b2a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381452} {:answer/id #uuid "d10dabab-6b74-599d-89d4-dd220a0c69b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a09b9e0a-fb52-572c-ba07-497a7045bfd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381451} :answer-field/id #uuid "a43d8aba-317d-57da-892e-c4a09867a982" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6099760-d85e-5730-93dd-a4f12f20c95b" :question/math-academy-id "q-171904" :question/problem "Let $m\\in N$ and $n\\in N.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $∀\\,n\\in N,\\,1\\le n.$\n- The truth set of the predicate $(\\,∀\\,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\\,)$ is $N.$\n- $∀\\,m,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∀\\,m,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ For all natural $m$ and $n,$ we have $m\\le n.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, since $1\\le n$ for all natural numbers $n.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For the predicate\n\n$$\nP(m):\\,∀\\,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\n$$\n\nwe note that $P(m)$ is true for some $m\\in N$ and false for others. For example, \n\n$$\nP(1):\\,∀\\,n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\n$$\n\nis true. However, \n\n$$\nP(2):\\,∀\\,n\\in N,\\,2\\le n\n$$\n\nis false, since $2≰1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∀\\,m,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\\,)\n$$\n\n \ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∀m\\in N\\,(\\,{{∀n\\in N,\\,m\\le n}_{⏟}}_{P(m)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(m)$ is true over the universal set $N.$ But, according to statement II, this is not the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152450 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152088 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "051e28ef-ac7f-5b29-bd93-c1e24a4bc699" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Free and Bound Occurrences of Variables" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050691 :topic/math-academy-id 2792 :topic/title "Nested Quantifiers"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9876711a-c97f-5b1f-9f76-9badba2e2ef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149232} {:answer/id #uuid "dc2c0f65-8d7d-50b1-8e39-ff63591a19c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149233} {:answer/id #uuid "ef041c1d-37c0-5738-b5d9-3323929dd32a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149234} {:answer/id #uuid "0995d81f-614e-558d-ab2b-f66448cb11e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 